---
import Layout from "@components/Layout.astro";
import PageHeader from "@components/PageHeader.astro";
import { Grid, Card, Alert } from "webcoreui/astro";
import DecklistTooltip from "@components/DecklistTooltip.astro";
---

<Layout title="Introduction - Replenish Guide">
    <PageHeader
        title="Replenish Guide"
        description="<strong>DON'T PANIC.</strong><br />Welcome, weary traveler. What you are looking at is the mostly harmless, yet wholly definitive guide to playing the Replenish deck in the Premodern format. This compendium is an expanded, interactive adaptation of the <a href='https://squeeshouseofpizza.com/en-brl/pages/replenish-primer-brian-siu' target='_blank' style='color: var(--w-color-primary-20); text-decoration: underline;'>excellent primer</a> originally penned by <strong>Brian Siu (Dr Siuss)</strong>. It has been redesigned and upgraded to serve as your ultimate resource—so grab your towel, ensure your tray table is securely stowed, and let's cheat some enchantments into play."
        imageSrc="/src/assets/banners/replenish.webp"
        imageAlt="Replenish"
        imageCaption="Replenish by Jim Nelson"
        isCard={false}
    />

    <div style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem; margin-top: 1rem;">
        <Alert theme="warning">
            <strong>Work In Progress:</strong> This site is currently under construction.
            It may contain unedited information, errors, missing card links, or sections
            that still need adjustment.
        </Alert>
    </div>

    <Card title="What is Replenish?" titleTag="h2">
        <p>
            <strong>Replenish</strong> is a premier combo-control archetype in Premodern Magic:
            The Gathering that leverages its namesake sorcery to cheat powerful, high-cost
            enchantments onto the battlefield directly from the graveyard. The core operating game plan
            is straightforward in concept: aggressively curate your graveyard using card selection engines
            like <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Attunement">Attunement</a> and <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Frantic Search">Frantic Search</a>, and then resolve <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Replenish">Replenish</a> to return
            them all at once for just four mana.
        </p>

        <h3 style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 1rem; font-size: 1.25rem;">
            The Core Combo: The Wave Lock
        </h3>
        <p style="margin-bottom: 0.75rem;">
            The deck's primary terminal condition revolves around a devastating
            stack synergy between two flagship enchantments:
        </p>
        <ul style="padding-left: 1.25rem; margin-bottom: 0;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
                <strong><a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Opalescence">Opalescence</a>:</strong> Transforms all of your non-Aura enchantments
                into creatures with power and toughness equal to their respective mana values.
            </li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
                <strong><a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Parallax Wave">Parallax Wave</a>:</strong> Ordinarily utilized to temporarily exile
                up to five target creatures. However, because <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Opalescence">Opalescence</a> animates Wave into
                a creature,
                <em>it can target itself</em>.
            </li>
        </ul>
        <p style="margin-top: 0.75rem; margin-bottom: 0;">
            By stacking four exile triggers targeting opposing battlefield presence and then
            using the final fading counter to exile the <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Parallax Wave">Parallax Wave</a> itself, Wave
            leaves play <em>before</em> its initial exile triggers resolve. Because its
            return-to-play clause triggers and resolves while nothing has been exiled yet, your opponent's creatures
            are exiled permanently once the remaining stack clears, and your Wave returns to the battlefield refreshed with
            five new counters. Incorporating <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Decree of Silence">Decree of Silence</a> into this loop permanently intercepts and counters
            every subsequent spell your opponent attempts to cast.
        </p>
    </Card>

    <Card title="Why Replenish?" titleTag="h2">
        <p>
            If you are seeking an archetype capable of delivering explosive, unilateral combo
            kills while possessing the stamina to grind through intense, grueling
            control matches via superior interaction and card selection,
            Replenish is your ideal match.
        </p>
        <ul style="padding-left: 1.25rem; margin-bottom: 3rem;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
                You relish combo architectures that reward disciplined setup, analytical patience, and explosive terminal payoffs.
            </li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
                You appreciate intricate decision trees, precise sequencing mechanics, and the tactical puzzle of navigating out of complex board states.
            </li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
                You value structural adaptability, seeking a framework that seamlessly transitions across combo, control, and midrange postures as match dynamics evolve.
            </li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
                You aim to decisively dominate local metagames heavily populated by Enchantress, slower midrange piles, and traditional permission control.
            </li>
        </ul>
    </Card>

    <Card title="Strengths and Weaknesses" titleTag="h2">
        <Grid
            column={{ md: 2 }}
            gap="md"
            style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 2rem;"
        >
            <Card>
                <h3 style="margin-top: 0; color: var(--w-color-success);">
                    Strengths
                </h3>
                <ul style="padding-left: 1.25rem;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
                        <strong>Explosive Wins:</strong> A single resolved <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Replenish">Replenish</a>
                        assembling the Opal-Wave-Decree lock almost universally terminates the game
                        on the spot.
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
                        <strong>Role Flexibility:</strong> Capable of seamlessly shifting between pure combo, hard control,
                        or aggressive midrange depending on matchup pressure. Proactive disruption pieces like <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Meddling Mage">Meddling Mage</a> solidify early board control.
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
                        <strong>Dominant Matchups:</strong> Statistically favored against
                        foundational Premodern pillars, including Enchantress, midrange piles,
                        creature beatdowns, and slow control variants.
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
                        <strong>Post-Ban Positioning:</strong> With <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Parallax Tide">Parallax Tide</a>
                        removed from competitive legalities, opposing blue decks no longer threaten instant land sweepers, liberating
                        critical sideboard and maindeck utility space for your own gameplan.
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </Card>

            <Card>
                <h3 style="margin-top: 0; color: var(--w-color-danger);">
                    Weaknesses
                </h3>
                <ul style="padding-left: 1.25rem;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
                        <strong>Graveyard Dependence:</strong> Inherently vulnerable
                        to hate pieces like <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Tormod's Crypt">Tormod's Crypt</a>, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Phyrexian Furnace">Phyrexian Furnace</a>, and
                        <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Planar Void">Planar Void</a> (though post-sideboard creature plans mitigate
                        this).
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
                        <strong>Multi-Axis Aggression:</strong> Vulnerable against
                        strategies generating dual-axis pressure simultaneously (e.g.,
                        a rapid clock supported by heavy mana denial or dense permission spells).
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
                        <strong>Faster Unfair Combos:</strong> Structurally challenged by ultra-fast linear engines like <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Phyrexian Devourer">Devourer Combo</a> that can manufacture lethal sequences faster than you can position meaningful early interaction.
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
                        <strong>Mana Hungry:</strong> Primary engine spells sit at three and four
                        mana, necessitating deliberate defensive navigation during early turns before your selection mechanics stabilize.
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </Card>
        </Grid>
    </Card>
    <DecklistTooltip />
</Layout>
